 text. \nAddress \nClick here to enter text. \nYour Authorised Officer \n(refer to clause 26 - Notices) \nName \nClick here to enter text. \nPosition \nClick here to enter text. \nAddress \nClick here to enter text. \nTelephone \nClick here to enter text. \nMobile \nClick here to enter text. \nE-mail  \nClick here to enter text. \nServices \nClick here to enter text. being the services described in further detail in \nSchedule A - Services. \nProject title \nClick here to enter text. \nKey Individual/s if required \n(refer to clause 4 ? Key \nIndividuals \nName: \nClick here to enter text. \nPosition: \nClick here to enter text. \nTelephone: \nClick here to enter text. \nEmail: \nClick here to enter text. \n\n \n? State of New South Wales \n4 \n \nCommencement Date of \nthis Agreement \n(refer to clause 2 - Term) \nClick here to enter text. \nExpiry Date \nClick here to enter text. \nLast Day that Further Period \noption can be exercised \n(refer to clause 2.3) \nInsert number of days before the Expiry Date \nFurther Period \nInsert extension term.  from the Expiry Date \nFee (refer to clause 8 ?\nPayment) \nThe maximum total Fee is $ Click here to enter text. (excluding GST), \npayable as set out in Schedule A ? Services.  \nEmail address for invoices \nClick here to enter text. \n \nSpecial Conditions (refer to \nclause 27.6 ? Inconsistency) \n \n \n \n\n \n? State of New South Wales \n5 \n \nTerms and Conditions \nDefinitions and Term \n1. \nInterpretation and Definitions \n1.1. \nInterpretation \nIn this Agreement, unless the context requires otherwise: \n(a) the capitalised terms set out in the Details have the meaning ascribed to them there; \n(b) where any time limit under this Agreement falls on a day which is not a Business Day, the \ntime limit will be deemed to have expired on the next Business Day; \n(c) a reference to a statute, regulation, ordinance or by-law will be deemed to include a \nreference to all statutes, regulations, ordinances or by-laws amending, consolidating or \nreplacing same from time to time; \n(d) specific examples do not limit the meaning of general words introduced by ?including? or \n?for example? or similar expressions; \n(e) monetary amounts are expressed in Australian dollars; \n(f) references to persons include bodies corporate, government agencies and vice versa; \n(g) references to the parties include references to respective directors, officers, employees \nand agents of the parties; \n(h) nothing in this Agreement is to be interpreted against a party solely on the grounds that \nthe party put forward this Agreement or any part of it; and \n(i) where an expression is defined, any other grammatical form of that expression has a \ncorresponding meaning. \n1.2. \nDefinitions \nAgreement means this agreement between you and the Department for you to provide the \nServices according to the Details, Terms and Conditions, Schedules and any documents \nreferenced in this Agreement. \nBackground IP means IP that a party makes available to the other party for purposes of this \nAgreement (including IP in relation to Background Material) which: \n(a) was in existence prior to the Commencement Date; or \n(b) is brought into existence independently of this Agreement; but \n(c) excludes IP owned by third parties.  \nBackground Material means material that a party provides to the other party to use for the \npurposes of this Agreement, which: \n(a) was in existence prior to the Commencement Date; or \n(b) is brought into existence independently of this Agreement; and \n(c) that the providing party owns or is licensed to use. \nBusiness Day means any day other than a Saturday, Sunday or public holiday in New South \nWales. \nClaim means any cost, expense, loss, damage, claim, action, proceeding or other liability \n(whether in contract, tort or otherwise), however arising and includes legal costs on a full \nindemnity basis. \n\n \n? State of New South Wales \n6 \n \nConfidential Information of a party means all information and data of whatever description \nand in whatever form which: \n(a) is by its nature confidential; \n(b) a party has designated as confidential; \n(c) is capable of protection at common law or equity as confidential information; or \n(d) is derived or produced partly from information described in paragraph (a), (b) or (c) above,   \nbut does not include information that: \n(a) is in the public domain; or \n(b) the receiving party independently knows or develops other than as a result of a breach of \nthis Agreement or any other obligation of confidentiality owed by or to any other person. \nContract IP means new IP created or brought into existence under this Agreement, and \nincludes IP relating to the Contract Material. \nContract Material means all documents, data and other material first created or brought into \nexistence under this Agreement and includes reports, questionnaires, laboratory notes, \nsoftware, technical information, plans, maps, charts, photographs, drawings, calculations, \ntables, biological material and data. \nCorrectly Rendered Invoice means an invoice that: \n(a) sets out the relevant party?s contact details including ABN; \n(b) specifies the amount to be paid, and whether or not this includes GST; \n(c) identifies this Agreement and any Milestones to which the payment applies; and \n(d) is sent to the email address specified in the Details or otherwise notified by the party to \nwhom it is addressed. \nData Breach means any suspected or actual access to, or disclosure of, information in your \npossession or control which includes data or Personal Information: \n(a) that the Department provided to you; or \n(b) that you have obtained in the course of providing the Services and Deliverables; \nunless such access or disclosure is authorised by this Agreement. \nDeliverables means all the goods, supplies or outcomes you must provide to the Department \nin the course of performing your obligations under this Agreement, including reports, \ndocuments and other materials embodying data, results and other information. Details are set \nout in Schedule A, and may be amended from time to time by the Department. \nGST Law means A New Tax System (Goods and Services Tax) Act 1999 (Cth) and related \nregulations and instruments. \nIntellectual Property or IP includes: \n(a) all present and future rights conferred by statute, common law or equity in relation to \ncopyright, inventions, plant varieties, trademarks, designs, patents; and \n(b) all other rights resulting from intellectual activity in the industrial, scientific, literary or \nartistic fields including trade secrets and know-how; \nbut does not include moral rights as defined in the Copyright Act 1968 (Cth). \nKPI means any performance standard or key performance indicator specified in Schedule A. \n\n \n? State of New South Wales \n7 \n \nLaw means any applicable: \n(a) statute, rule, regulation, by-law or statutory instrument of the Commonwealth, any State \nor Territory, or local government area; \n(b) order, request or direction issued by a regulator or other authority; or \n(c) other requirement having the force of law; \nas amended, consolidated or replaced from time to time. \nMilestones means the stages at which you must complete parts of the Services as set out in \nSchedule A. \nModern Slavery has the same meaning as it has in the Modern Slavery Act 2018 (Cth). \nNotice means any approval, consent, instruction, order, direction, statement, request or \ncertificate, or other communication one party gives to another party under this Agreement. \nPersonal Information means personal information, as defined in the Privacy Law, which the \nDepartment provides or that you collect in connection with this Agreement. \nPrivacy Law means the Law concerning use of personal information in NSW, the \nCommonwealth or another Australian jurisdiction, as applicable. \nSigning Date means the date that the last party signed this Agreement. \nWHS Law means, as applicable: \n(a) the Work Health and Safety Act 2011, the Work Health and Safety Act 2011 (Cth) or \nequivalent legislation in the relevant jurisdiction; and  \n(b) the regulations, codes of practice and other instruments under the applicable Act. \n2. \nTerm \n2.1. \nThis Agreement will commence on the Commencement Date and will continue for the Term. \n2.2. \nIf the Commencement Date predates the Signing Date, the parties: \n(a) acknowledge that one or both parties started the activities contemplated in this \nAgreement on the Commencement Date, despite this Agreement not being finalised and \nsigned until the Signing Date; and \n(b) agree that this Agreement is deemed to have applied to all such activities from the \nCommencement Date. \n2.3. \nIf stated in the Details, the Department has an option to renew this Agreement for the \nFurther Period, provided that: \n(a) it serves on you a Notice of exercise of option not later than the Last Day; and \n(b) at the time of service, you have complied with or fully remedied all of your obligations to \nthe Department?s satisfaction. \n2.4. \nDuring the Further Period, the same terms and conditions will apply except for: \n(a) adjustment to the Expiry Date; and \n(b) adjustment of the Fee, if any.  \n\n \n? State of New South Wales \n8 \n \nWhat you must do \n3. \nYour general obligations \n3.1. \nYou must: \n(a) perform the Services and provide the Deliverables as an independent contractor in \naccordance with this Agreement, including: \n \nall work reasonably incidental to provision of the Services and Deliverables; and \n \nany related documentation; \n(b) perform your obligations with due professional care and skill and in a cost effective \nmanner, including in accordance with any KPIs; \n(c) complete any agreed Milestone in accordance with this Agreement by the required date \nand at any times, intervals and frequency specified in Schedule A; \n(d) do all that is necessary to complete the Deliverables to ensure that they are fit for the \npurposes required by this Agreement and any purposes reasonably inferred; and  \n(e) maintain sufficient capability and resources to meet your obligations under this \nAgreement. \nCompliance with Laws and policies \n3.2. \nYou must: \n(a) obtain at your own cost all licences, approvals and consents necessary to meet your \nobligations under this Agreement; \n(b) comply with the Privacy Law, the WHS Law and all other Laws that are relevant to the \nDeliverables, this Agreement, or your registration as an entity; \n(c) comply with the NSW Government Supplier Code of Conduct and all policies, guidelines \nand reasonable directions the Department provides to you (and take reasonable steps to \ninform yourself of the Department?s requirements); and \n(d) when carrying out activities under this Agreement: \n \nnot cause damage to property and the environment, and make good any such \ndamage at your expense; and \n \nprevent nuisance or inconvenience to anyone who may be affected when \nperforming your obligations under this Agreement. \n3.3. \nIf required by the Department, you must provide a subcontractor?s statement for the \npurposes of section 175B of the Workers Compensation Act 1987, Schedule 2 Part 5 of the \nPayroll Tax Act 2007 and section 127 of the Industrial Relations Act 1996. \nObligation to correct failures \n3.4. \nWithout limiting the Department?s rights at law or under this Agreement, if you fail to provide \nany agreed Service or Deliverable in accordance with the Schedules, or fail to meet a KPI, or if \nyou otherwise breach this Agreement (Failure), you must at no additional cost to the \nDepartment: \n(a) immediately correct the issue which caused the Failure;  \n(b) if the Department requests, re-provide the Deliverables that gave rise to the Failure; \n\n \n? State of New South Wales \n9 \n \n(c) arrange all additional resources reasonably necessary to provide the Deliverables in \naccordance with this Agreement; and \n(d) if the Department requests, provide a detailed report on the steps you have taken to \nprevent similar future Failures. \n3.5. \nIf you rectify the issue which caused a Failure, this will not affect the Department?s rights in \nconnection with that Failure, create a waiver of the Department?s rights or any future rights \nin relation to that Failure, nor estop the Department from relying on the terms of this \nAgreement to their full effect. \n4. \nKey Individuals \n4.1. \nIf Key Individuals are nominated in the Details, you must: \n(a) ensure that the Key Individuals carry out the Services unless you have obtained written \nconsent from the Department; and  \n(b) ensure that the Key Individuals are competent and have the necessary skills to perform \nthe Services. \n4.2. \nIf any of the Key Individuals are not available to perform the Services allocated to them, you \nmust immediately: \n(a) notify the Department of the circumstances; and \n(b) if the Department requests, arrange for replacement of that individual with a person with \nsimilar expertise and experience satisfactory to the Department, at no cost to the \nDepartment. \n4.3. \nIf replacement of the relevant Key Individual is not possible, the Department may give you \nnotice of termination under clause 21, or reduce the scope of this Agreement under clause \n23. \n5. \nSubcontractors \n5.1. \nYou must not subcontract the performance of any part of your obligations without the \nDepartment?s prior written consent. The Department may refuse to give such consent, or \nmay give consent on such terms and conditions as it thinks fit in its absolute discretion. \n5.2. \nIf you subcontract the performance of any part of the Services: \n(a) you must engage any subcontractors under written agreement on terms consistent with \nthis Agreement;  \n(b) you must use your best endeavours to ensure the subcontractors comply with the WHS \nLaw and hold insurance as specified in clause 18; \n(c) you remain fully responsible for provision of the Services; and \n(d) the Department may at any time request from you a copy of your agreement with a \nsubcontractor and request reasonable amendments to ensure it is consistent with this \nAgreement. \n6. \nModern Slavery \n6.1. \nYou must take reasonable steps to identify, assess and address risks of Modern Slavery \npractices in the operations and supply chains used in the production of the Deliverables.  \n\n \n? State of New South Wales \n10 \n \n6.2. \nIf at any time you become aware of Modern Slavery practices in the operations and supply \nchains you use in providing the Services, you must as soon as reasonably practicable, take all \nreasonable action to address or remove these practices, including where relevant by \naddressing any practices of other entities in your supply chains. \n7. \nWork health and safety  \n7.1. \nYou must: \n(a) ensure the health and safety of all people who your activities may affect under this \nAgreement, including your personnel and subcontractors and members of the public, as \nrequired by the WHS Law; \n(b) make your own risk assessment and analysis of all work health and safety risks associated \nwith providing the Deliverables; and \n(c) develop and use safe systems of work including safe work method statements or safe \nwork procedures. \n7.2. \nYou warrant and represent that you: \n(a) have the necessary resources to comply with the WHS Law;  \n(b) have taken appropriate steps to identify reasonably foreseeable health and safety hazards \nin relation to provision of the Deliverables; and  \n(c) have taken all necessary measures to assess and eliminate or control the risks arising from \nthose hazards. \n7.3. \nYou must promptly provide any work, health and safety documentation concerning the \nDeliverables to the Department on request. \nAbout Payments  \n8. \nPayment conditions \n8.1. \nThe Department will pay you the Fee on the condition that: \n(a) you give the Department any Deliverable, and complete any Milestone, that is due to be \ndelivered or completed to the Department?s satisfaction; \n(b) the Department receives a Correctly Rendered Invoice from you that clearly identifies the \nServices completed and the Fees claimed; and  \n(c) you provide any additional information the Department requests so it may determine \nwhether or not the amount is payable. \n8.2. \nThe Department will pay a Correctly Rendered Invoice within 30 calendar days (or 5 Business \nDays if you are a registered small business under the NSW Government?s Faster Payment \nTerms Policy) of the Department receiving all information requested under clause 8.1. \n8.3. \nIf the Department pays any of the Fee under clause 8.2 this does not constitute an admission \nby the Department that the Services have conformed with this Agreement and no payment \nwill be deemed to release you from your obligations under this Agreement. \n\n \n? State of New South Wales \n11 \n \n8.4. \nThe Department may set off any amount or liability owed by you, whether or not due for \npayment, against any money due for payment by the Department to you under any \nagreement, understanding or arrangement. \n9. \nWhat the Fee includes   \n9.1. \nThe Fee includes all fees, costs, expenses, charges and disbursements you incur in providing \nthe Services and Deliverables.  \n9.2. \nIf you are an individual and deemed to be an employee for the purposes of superannuation, \nthe parties agree that the Fees are inclusive of any compulsory superannuation contribution \nat the rate payable under the Superannuation Guarantee (Administration) Act 1992 (Cth) or \nany related laws and any such contribution amount will be paid into a fund you nominate. \n9.3. \nThe Department is not liable to pay you any additional costs above the Fee. \n10. \nGST \n10.1. Words and expressions used in this clause which are defined in the GST Law have the same \nmeanings given to them in the GST Law. \n10.2. All consideration for any supply under this Agreement is exclusive of any GST in relation to \nthe supply, unless otherwise stated. \n10.3. If: \n(a) despite any other provision of this Agreement, GST is imposed on a supply made under \nthis Agreement; and \n(b) a party is or will be entitled to receive an input tax credit (as defined in the GST Law) in \nrelation to that supply, \nthat party will pay an additional amount equal to the GST imposed on that supply, at the time \nand in the manner specified in this Agreement, unless payment is otherwise payable under \nthis Agreement in relation to that supply. \n10.4. You must be registered under the GST Law at the time of making any supply under this \nAgreement on which GST is imposed. If you are not registered under the GST Law, you will \nnot be entitled to receive any additional amount as provided under this clause. \n10.5. If for any reason the Department pays you an amount under this clause which is more than \nthe GST imposed on the supply, you must repay the excess to the Department on demand or \nthe Department may set off the excess against any other amounts due to you. \nIntellectual Property and Information \n11. \nIntellectual Property \n11.1. All Contract IP vests in you on creation, unless otherwise stated in the Special Conditions. \n11.2. You grant the Department a perpetual, transferable, royalty free licence to use the Contract \nIP, including a right to grant sub-licences. \n\n \n? State of New South Wales \n12 \n \n11.3. Each party retains ownership of its Background Material and Background IP. \n11.4. Each party grants to the other party a worldwide, free, perpetual, irrevocable, non-exclusive \nlicence to use its Background Material (including a right to grant sub-licences): \n(a) for the purposes of this Agreement; and  \n(b) to the extent it forms part of the Contract Material, in accordance with that party?s rights \nto use the Contract Material. \n11.5. You warrant that: \n(a) to your actual knowledge or belief, after actual enquiry, you own or are otherwise entitled \nto provide the Background Material for the purposes of this Agreement; \n(b) except to the extent you disclose in this Agreement, or to the Department when making \nBackground Material available, your Background IP is unencumbered; and \n(c) you will not deal with your Background IP in a manner that restricts the Department?s \nexercise of its rights under this Agreement. \n11.6. Each party must immediately advise the other if it becomes aware of any infringement of the \nother party?s Background IP. Each party must give the other party all assistance which it may \nreasonably request in order to protect its Background IP, provided the owner pays the other \nparty?s reasonable costs and expenses of doing so. \n12. \nConfidential Information  \n12.1. Each party must keep the other party?s Confidential Information confidential and not disclose \nit to any other person without the prior written consent of the disclosing party, subject to \nclause 12.2. \n12.2. Each party may disclose the other party?s Confidential Information: \n(a) to its contractors as reasonably required for purposes of this Agreement; \n(b)  to its legal and professional advisors for the purpose of obtaining professional advice; or  \n(c) as required or authorised by Law or Parliament;  \nprovided the party ensures that the recipient keeps the Confidential Information confidential. \n12.3. The Department may disclose your Confidential Information if required to do so by law or by \nParliament, provided that: \n(a) it gives you prior notice with full details of the circumstances and the information to be \ndisclosed; and  \n(b) it identifies such information as confidential. \n13. \nUse of names and publication \n13.1. The parties agree that the Department will take sole responsibility for all media \nannouncements regarding the Services and Deliverables.  You must refer any media enquiries \nregarding the Project or the Services to the Department. \n13.2. You must obtain written consent from the Department before you use the Department?s \nname or any derivative thereof, or any trademark or logo of the Department. Any use of the \nDepartment?s logo must comply with the NSW Government Brand Guidelines. \n\n \n? State of New South Wales \n13 \n \n13.3. You must not publish or otherwise disclose the results of the Services or any Contract \nMaterial except with the written approval of the Department or as otherwise permitted in \naccordance with this Agreement. \n13.4. Where the purpose of the Project requires or encourages the dissemination of information to \nthe public, you may publish academic, scientific or technical articles relating to the Contract \nMaterial, provided that: \n(a) such publication does not disclose any Confidential Information or Personal Information;  \n(b) such publication does not prejudice any future registration of, or commercial exploitation \nof, the Contract Material and Contract IP; \n(c) any publication or presentation relating or referring to the Contract Material or Contract \nIP includes an acknowledgement of the Department?s ownership; and \n(d) you provide a copy of all such publications to the Department. \n14. \nPrivacy and data \n14.1. You must: \n(a) ensure that Personal Information is used only for the purposes of this Agreement and is \nprotected against loss and against unauthorised access, use, modification or disclosure or \nother misuse; \n(b) not transfer Personal Information outside New South Wales or access it, or allow it to be \naccessed, from outside New South Wales unless expressly permitted in Schedule A; \n(c) not disclose any Personal Information (unless you are required or authorised by law to do \nso) without the written consent of the individual to whom the Personal Information \nrelates, or the Department; \n(d) comply with the Privacy Laws; \n(e) comply with the Information Protection Principles applying to NSW public sector agencies \nunder the Privacy and Personal Information Protection Act 1998 in relation to Personal \nInformation as if you were an agency directly subject to that Act; and \n(f) include equivalent requirements to this clause (Privacy and data) in any subcontract you \nenter for the provision of any of the Services or Deliverables under this Agreement. \n14.2. \nIf you become aware of a Data Breach, you must: \n(a) immediately report it to the Department?s Authorised Officer and by email to \ngipa@dpird.nsw.gov.au; and \n(b) fully cooperate with the Department in responding to any such breach. \n15. \nPersonal Information you provide to the Department \n15.1. Where you provide the Department with any Personal Information, including your own \nPersonal Information if you are an individual then, subject to clause 15.2: \n(a) you authorise the Department to make such Personal Information (including name, \naddress and details of work performance) available to other State, Commonwealth and \nlocal government agencies and funding agencies for reporting, statistical and accounting \npurposes; and \n(b) you warrant that you have the permission of the individuals concerned to authorise such \ndisclosure. \n\n \n? State of New South Wales \n14 \n \n15.2. If you wish to: \n(a) withhold authorisation of the Department to disclose Personal Information; or \n(b) obtain details of any Personal Information about you the Department holds; \nyou must provide the relevant details to the Department?s Authorised Officer in writing. \n16. \nDisclosure of Information \n16.1. You acknowledge that, under the Government Information (Public Access) Act 2009, the \nDepartment may be required to publicly disclose information about this Agreement at \nhttps://tenders.nsw.gov.au. None of the disclosure obligations require the disclosure of: \n(a) the commercial-in-confidence provisions of a contract; \n(b) any matter that could reasonably be expected to affect public safety or security; or \n(c) information which would be exempt from disclosure if it were the subject of an \napplication under the Government Information (Public Access) Act 2009. \n16.2. You may nominate any items you consider are confidential and why, to assist the Department \nin determining what items to disclose. \n17. \nAccess to Information \n17.1. If this Agreement involves you providing any services to the public on behalf of the \nDepartment, you must provide the Department with immediate access to any of the \nfollowing information contained in records you hold, at your own expense, within seven (7) \ndays of receiving a written request: \n(a) information that relates directly to the performance of the Services; \n(b) information you have collected from members of the public to whom you provide, or offer \nto provide, such Services; and \n(c) information you receive from the Department to enable you to provide such Services. \n17.2. For the purposes of this clause (Access to Information), information does not include: \n(a) information that discloses or would tend to disclose your financing arrangements, \nfinancial modelling, cost structure or profit margin; \n(b) information that you are prohibited from disclosing to the Department by provision made \nby or under any Act, whether of any State or Territory, or of the Commonwealth; or \n(c) information that, if disclosed to the Department, could reasonably be expected to place \nYou at a substantial commercial disadvantage in relation to the Department, whether at \npresent or in the future. \nDealing with Risk \n18. \nInsurance  \n18.1. You must maintain, at your own cost, during the term of this Agreement: \n(a) a broadform public liability policy of insurance to the value of at least $10 million in \nrespect of each claim and unlimited in the aggregate as to the number of occurrences in \nthe policy period; \n\n \n? State of New South Wales \n15 \n \n(b) workers? compensation insurance as required by all relevant laws of Australia relating to \nworkers compensation; and \n(c) if the Services involve providing professional advice, a professional indemnity insurance to \nthe value of at least $2 million in respect of each claim and in the aggregate as to the \nnumber of occurrences in the policy period, and you must maintain that policy for a \nperiod of 7 years from the end of this Agreement; and \n(d) any additional insurance the Department requires in writing. \n18.2. You must not do or allow any act, matter or thing or omission whereby any of the policies \nreferred to in this clause may be vitiated, rendered void or voidable. \n18.3. If requested by the Department, you must provide a copy of valid and current certificates of \ncurrency for each or any of the policies described above which include the policy number, \nexpiry date, level of cover (per claim and aggregate), policy excess, summary of cover \n(including exclusions and endorsements) and jurisdictional limits. \n18.4. Without limiting clause 18.1, each party warrants that it has and will maintain appropriate \ninsurance to cover any liability it may incur in relation to this Agreement.   \n19. \nIndemnities \n19.1. You must indemnify and keep indemnified the Department, the Crown in right of the State of \nNew South Wales and their officers, employees and agents against any loss (paid or payable \nincluding legal costs and expenses on a solicitor/own client basis) or liability incurred by, or \nmade against, any of those indemnified arising directly or indirectly from any Claim by any \nperson in connection with: \n(a) the Services or the use of any Deliverables; \n(b) your breach of this Agreement including any failure to provide the Services or \nDeliverables; \n(c) your or your subcontractor?s or employee?s unlawful or negligent act or omission in \nconnection with this Agreement; \n(d) any illness, injury or death of any person you, or your subcontractors or employees, cause \nor contribute to in connection with this Agreement; \n(e) any loss or damage to real or personal property you, or your subcontractors or employees, \ncause in connection with this Agreement; or \n(f) you or your subcontractor?s acts or omissions in connection with this Agreement that is in \ninfringement of any Intellectual Property, or privacy rights of the Department or any third \nparty. \n19.2. Your liability to indemnify the Department under this clause: \n(a) will be reduced proportionately to the extent that the Department?s negligent or unlawful \nacts or omissions, or those of its officers, employees or agents, contributed to the relevant \nloss or liability; and \n(b) does not exclude or reduce the liability of, or benefit to, a party that may arise by \noperation of the common law, statute or the other terms of this Agreement. \n\n \n? State of New South Wales \n16 \n \n20. \nLiability Caps  \n20.1. Your aggregate liability to the Department under this Agreement whether in contract, tort \n(including negligence), statute or any other cause of action is limited to an amount equal to \nfive (5) times the total Fee, subject to clause 20.3. \n20.2. The Department?s aggregate liability to you under this Agreement whether in contract, tort \n(including negligence), statute or any other cause of action, is limited to one (1) times the \ntotal Fee, subject to clause 20.3. \n20.3. Nothing in this Agreement limits or excludes: \n(a) liability that cannot by law be limited or excluded; \n(b) an amount payable under an insurance policy taken out in conjunction with this \nAgreement; \n(c) the liability of either party in respect of personal injury (including sickness and death) or \nreal or tangible property loss or damage;  \n(d) liability of a party in respect of a breach of the clauses concerning Confidential \nInformation, or Privacy and data;  \n(e) Your liability under clause 12 (Intellectual Property); \n(f) liability of either party for unlawful or illegal acts or conduct; or \n(g) the Department?s liability to pay such of the Fee as is due and payable. \nReduction in Services and Ending this Agreement \n21. \nTermination \n21.1. Where a party has breached this Agreement, the other party may by Notice: \n(a) require that party to rectify the breach within 30 days of the Notice, and terminate this \nAgreement immediately by further Notice if they fail to do so; or \n(b) if the breach cannot be remedied, terminate this Agreement immediately. \n21.2. The Department may terminate this Agreement by Notice, with effect on the date stated in \nthe Notice, if: \n(a) you breach this Agreement in a manner that, in the Department?s opinion, is not capable \nof remedy; \n(b) you breach any of the following provisions: clause 3 (Your General Obligations); clause 4 \n(Key Individuals); clause 6 (Modern Slavery); clause 7 (Work Health and Safety); clause 12 \n(Confidential Information); clause 18 (Insurance), or clause 27.10 (Assignment); \n(c) the Department considers that provision of the Services and Deliverables is no longer \nviable; \n(d) the Department considers that there has been a material change in circumstances in your \nfinancial position, your structure or your identity; or \n(e) you become insolvent, if you are the subject of a debtors or creditors petition under the \nBankruptcy Act 1966 (Cth), or if you resolve to go into administration or liquidation or \nhave a summons for your winding up presented to a Court or enter into any scheme of \narrangement with your creditors.  \n\n \n? State of New South Wales \n17 \n \n21.3. This clause does not exclude or reduce the rights of a party to terminate this Agreement \narising by operation of the common law or statute or the other terms of this Agreement. \n22. \nConsequences of termination  \n22.1. On termination or expiry of this Agreement: \n(a) accrued rights and obligations are not affected;  \n(b) you must deliver to the Department all Contract Material not delivered to date, including \nincomplete material; and \n(c) all Background Material and Confidential Material must be returned to the party that \nprovided it, or disposed of as that party directs. \n23. \nReduction in Scope \n23.1. Without limiting clause 21 (Termination), the Department may provide Notice to you to \nreduce the scope of the Services or Deliverables at the Department?s convenience, with effect \nfrom the date stated in the Notice. The Department's liability to pay the Fees abates in \naccordance with the reduction in scope. \n24. \nWhat you must do if you receive a Notice \n24.1. Upon receipt of a Notice of termination or reduction you must: \n(a) stop or reduce work as specified in the Notice; \n(b) take all available steps to minimise loss resulting from that termination; and \n(c) continue work on any part of the Services not affected by the Notice. \nOther Legal Matters \n25. \nDispute Resolution \n25.1. If a dispute arises in relation to this Agreement (?a Dispute?), a party must comply with this \nclause before starting arbitration or court proceedings except proceedings for urgent \ninterlocutory relief.  \n25.2. A party claiming that a Dispute has arisen must notify the other party giving details of the \ndispute (Dispute Notice) in accordance with the requirements of clause 26 (Notices). \n25.3. Following receipt of a Dispute Notice, each party must refer the Dispute to a senior \nrepresentative, who: \n(a) does not have prior direct involvement in the Dispute; and \n(b) has authority to negotiate and settle the Dispute. \n25.4. If the Dispute is not resolved within 10 Business Days, from the date the Dispute Notice is \nreceived by the party to whom the Dispute Notice is given, the party which gave the Dispute \nNotice must refer the Dispute for mediation by the Australian Disputes Centre (ADC) for \nresolution in accordance with the mediation rules of the ADC. \n25.5. If the Dispute is not resolved within 40 Business Days after referral to mediation either party \nmay initiate proceedings in court. \n\n \n? State of New South Wales \n18 \n \n25.6. Each party must pay its own costs of complying with this clause and split the costs of the \nmediator evenly. \n26. \nNotices \n26.1. All Notices to be given under this Agreement must be in writing, and hand-delivered or \nemailed to the Authorised Officer specified in the Details. \n26.2. The receiving party will be deemed to have received the Notice as follows: \n(a) if hand delivered, on the day on which it is delivered or left at the relevant address; \n(b) if sent by email before 5.00pm on a Business Day, the first of the following occurring: \n \nwhen the sender receives an automated message confirming delivery; or \n \nfour hours after the time sent (as recorded on the device from which the sender \nsent the email) unless the sender receives an automated message that the email \nhas not been delivered. \n(c) if sent by email after 5pm on a Business Day or on a day that is not a Business Day, then it \nwill be deemed to be received on the next Business Day. \n26.3. Any such mode of service will be in all respects valid notwithstanding that the party on whom \nservice is affected may be in liquidation, bankruptcy or wound up and notwithstanding any \nother matter or event whatsoever. \n26.4. If you appoint a new Authorised Officer, you must Notify the Department of the details of \nyour new Authorised Officer within 7 days of the date of that change.  \n27. \nGeneral \n27.1. Survival: The following clauses survive termination or expiry of this Agreement: clauses \nconcerning Intellectual Property, Confidential Information, Privacy, Insurance, Indemnities, \nLiability Caps, Termination, Keeping of records, Dispute Resolution, this clause (Survival) and \nany other clause which by its nature is intended to survive this Agreement. \n27.2. Keeping of records and rights of access to such records \n(a) You must keep complete and accurate records and books of account with respect to your \nperformance of the Services (the ?Records?), and must retain such Records for a minimum \nof seven (7) years after expiry or termination of this Agreement; \n(b) You authorise the Department and any State or Commonwealth Government department \nor agency (the ?Auditors?) that has provided moneys to the Department for the purposes \nof the Project to which the Services relate, to examine and inspect, at reasonable times \nand on reasonable Notice, any Contract Material you hold, and allow any such Records to \nbe copied; and \n(c) You must provide all reasonable assistance in order for the Auditors to properly carry out \nthe inspections and audits referred to in this clause. \n27.3. Conflict of Interest \nYou must not: \n\n \n? State of New South Wales \n19 \n \n(a) carry on, or be involved in any capacity in, an activity or business which may conflict with, \nor adversely affect, your ability to carry out your obligations under this Agreement; or \n(b) act as a contractor to a person who owns or is involved in any capacity in an activity or \nbusiness which may conflict with, or adversely affect, your ability to carry out your \nobligations under this Agreement. \nYou agree to notify the Department immediately if a risk of such a conflict arises, and to \ncooperate with the Department in addressing any such conflict. \n27.4. Entire agreement: This Agreement states all the express terms the parties have agreed on. It \nsupersedes all prior contracts, obligations, representations, conduct and understandings. \n27.5. Variation: This Agreement may only be varied by agreement in writing including by an \nexchange of emails confirming the agreed variation. \n27.6. Inconsistency: If there is any inconsistency between provisions in this Agreement then the \norder of precedence will be: \n(a) the Details; then \n(b) any Special Conditions; then \n(c) these Terms and Conditions; then \n(d) any Schedules; then \n(e) any annexures or attachments. \n27.7. Negation of employment, partnership or agency: This Agreement does not create a \nrelationship of agency, partnership, and/or employment between the parties. \nYou must not represent yourself as being an employee or agent of the Department or as \notherwise able to bind or represent the Department. \n27.8. Severance: If any part of this Agreement is held to be invalid or ineffective, that part is \nremoved from this Agreement.  If that happens, it does not affect the validity of what \nremains. \n27.9. Waiver: If a party fails to exercise any of its rights under this Agreement, or delays exercising \nthose rights, that failure or delay will not operate as a waiver of those rights or any future \nrights or in any respect estop a party from relying on the terms of this Agreement to their full \nforce and effect. \nAny waiver by a party of a breach of this Agreement must be in writing and will not be \nconstrued as a waiver of any further breach of the same or any other provision. \n27.10. Assignment: You must not assign, novate or otherwise deal with any of your rights, \nobligations or interests under this Agreement, without the Department?s prior written \nconsent. \n27.11. Counterparts: This Agreement may be signed in any number of counterparts which taken \ntogether will constitute one instrument. \n27.12. Electronic execution: Each party agrees that the other may execute this Agreement \nelectronically as provided for in the Electronic Transactions Act 2000.  \n\n \n? State of New South Wales \n20 \n \n27.13. Governing Law: The laws of New South Wales govern this Agreement and the parties submit \nto the non-exclusive jurisdiction of the courts in that State. \n \n\n \n? State of New South Wales \n21 \n \nExecution Clauses \nDepartment \nSigned for and on behalf of the Crown in right of the State of New South Wales acting through the \nDepartment by its authorised signatory but not so as to incur personal liability: \nType name of Departmental manager with \nfinancial delegation \n \n \n \n \n \n \nSignature \n 